WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.421
Query-influencing data can enter a .

2
00:00:02.421 --> 00:00:13.384
NET application through route values, query strings, forms, JSON bodies, headers, files, queues, imports, logs, configuration, databases, and third-party services.

3
00:00:13.384 --> 00:00:16.411
Some of that data is obviously user-provided.

4
00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:23.271
Some appears later as stored records, partner data, operational metadata, or internal workflow values.

5
00:00:23.271 --> 00:00:27.441
All of it deserves review when it influences a database query.

6
00:00:27.441 --> 00:00:31.476
Stored or internal data should not automatically be trusted.

7
00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:44.591
A search term saved yesterday, a report filter imported from a file, a status value received from a partner, or a configuration value supplied by deployment can still affect query behavior later.

8
00:00:44.591 --> 00:00:51.182
Storage changes where the data lives; it does not prove the data is safe to mix into command text.

9
00:00:51.182 --> 00:01:01.742
Trust boundaries exist between web clients, APIs, backend services, background jobs, data stores, administrators, reporting tools, and maintenance utilities.

10
00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:09.409
Query construction is one of those boundaries because it translates application intent into database instructions.

11
00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:15.395
That boundary should be narrow, explicit, and protected by approved data-access patterns.

12
00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:24.542
Dynamic search, filtering, sorting, reporting, export, and administrative tools often deserve more review than simple lookup operations.

13
00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:29.922
They tend to accept flexible options and may touch sensitive or broad data sets.

14
00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:40.684
Flexibility is not automatically unsafe, but the application should control which query shapes are possible rather than letting callers define structure freely.

15
00:01:40.684 --> 00:01:46.669
A practical prevention technique is mapping data flow from entry point to query boundary.

16
00:01:46.669 --> 00:01:58.372
Identify where values enter, where they are normalized or validated, which model carries them, which service constructs the query, and which library sends it to the database.

17
00:01:58.372 --> 00:02:04.089
This map helps reviewers distinguish parameter values from query structure decisions.

18
00:02:04.089 --> 00:02:07.855
Data-flow mapping also helps teams find neglected paths.

19
00:02:07.855 --> 00:02:21.374
Background imports, scheduled reports, support dashboards, and legacy admin screens may not receive the same attention as public controllers, but they can still influence important database operations.

20
00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:27.427
Prevention is stronger when the review follows data rather than only following web routes.
